              "id": "GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828",
              "shortDescription": {
                 "text": "high severity - GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828 Uncaught Exception vulnerability in pkg:npm\/body-parser@1.19.0"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Impact\n\nA single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nPass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "For more information see [GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828)\n\n\nIf this is a false positive - consider using the HTML report to generate a suppression file.\nFor more information see [How dependency-check works](https://dependency-check.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/internals.html),\n[How to read the HTML report](https://dependency-check.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/thereport.html), and\n[Suppressing false positives](https://dependency-check.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/suppression.html)."
              },
